WEBVTT

t1.1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.767
Unified LLM effects.

t1.2
00:00:01.767 --> 00:00:09.200
The doeff-llm package defines the effects of talking to a language model once, for every provider.

t2
00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:17.600
LLM Chat, LLM Streaming Chat, LLM Structured Query and LLM Embedding name what the program asks for.

t3
00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:24.550
The provider packages, OpenAI, Gemini and OpenRouter, bring only handlers that answer them.

s1
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:30.800
Before doeff-llm, each provider package defined its own, near-identical effects.

s2
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:37.700
Those names remain as deprecated aliases: subclasses of LLM Chat that warn when they are built.

s3
00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:44.400
Now one program yields the same LLM Chat. Only the model name says which provider should answer.

s4
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:53.950
Under the same handlers, OpenAI answers gpt-4o, and Gemini answers gemini 1.5 pro.

k1.1
00:00:54.500 --> 00:01:00.533
Here is the page's example: one workflow, three models, and three provider handlers in one stack.

k1.2
00:01:00.533 --> 00:01:04.400
Mock handlers stand in for the real ones, so no API is called.

k2.1
00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.783
Each handler reads effect dot model.

k2.2
00:01:07.783 --> 00:01:14.500
The structured query for gpt-4o reaches OpenAI first, which supports it, and answers.

k3.1
00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:18.433
The chat for gemini 1.5 pro is not OpenAI's.

k3.2
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:24.200
The handler yields Pass, with k, and the next handler out, Gemini, answers.

k4
00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:31.700
Mistral large passes OpenAI and Gemini, and reaches OpenRouter, the catch-all on the outside.

f1
00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:41.100
A handler's annotation filters effects too. chat only is annotated effect colon LLM Chat.

f2
00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:48.100
The structured query does not match, so it skips chat only without a call, and any model answers it.

f3
00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:53.400
The LLM Chat matches. chat only is called, and answers it.

o1
00:01:55.150 --> 00:02:00.750
So the order of the stack matters. OpenRouter answers a chat for any model.

o2
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:10.150
Install it innermost, and it answers all three effects. OpenAI and Gemini never see one.

o3
00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:17.200
The catch-all goes outermost, and every handler inside it passes on the models it does not support.

z1.1
00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:20.650
One effect says what the program asks for.

z1.2
00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:25.700
The handlers decide who answers, by handling it, or passing it on with k.
